e early days of treating leukemia and even HIV, aggressive plasma exchange and leukapheresis were tried repeatedly. They consistently showed the same pattern: a brief, temporary drop in disease burden, followed by a swift rebound. Doctors abandoned them as *curative* tools decades ago. Modern medicine still uses occasional exchange transfusions for select emergencies (sickle cell crisis, malaria, hyperleukocytosis), but always as a bridge to definitive treatment (chemotherapy, antivirals, transplant), not as repeated standalone cures. To borrow your fish tank analogy: if the fish’s own body is leaking poison, no number of water changes can save it—the water will be dirty again before you finish the change. You have to treat the fish itself.
