ause if you don’t destroy the original bone marrow and immune system, the new donor stem cells would be immediately rejected and destroyed. You have to wipe out the old to make space for the new. That’s why the procedure is so dangerous and is reserved only for life-threatening blood cancers, not as a routine “blood replacement” for HIV or other conditions. So, when you hear about a leukemia patient or an HIV patient being cured after a “transplant,” they didn’t just get a big bag of clean blood. They underwent a near-death experience to first eliminate every last trace of their own blood-making machinery, and then got a new one. It’s the ultimate factory recall, not a fluid change. Your instinct—that replacing the entire system might cure the disease—is exactly what modern medicine realized decades ago. But the execution is light-years beyond a simple blood transfusion. It’s a total body reset, and it works only because it completely removes the hidden factories and reservoirs, not just the fluid in the pipes.
